# Strange loop

A **strange loop** is a structure in which moving upward or downward through
the levels of an apparent hierarchy unexpectedly returns to the level where the
movement began. The term was introduced by Douglas Hofstadter in *Gödel,
Escher, Bach* (1979) and developed further in *I Am a Strange Loop* (2007). It
is an interpretive notion, not a defined class of mathematical objects: what
makes a loop strange is that it crosses levels of description, not that it
goes round in a circle.

## Description

A strange loop needs three ingredients.

1. **Levels.** There is a hierarchy in which one level describes, represents
   or produces another: symbols and what they stand for, a drawing and the hand
   that draws it, a theory and statements about that theory.
2. **A crossing.** Something at one level acts as, or refers to, something at
   another level. The separation between the thing described and the
   description gives way.
3. **A return.** Following the crossings leads back to the starting point, so
   the system ends up as an object of its own representation.

Hofstadter's canonical examples each supply all three.

- **Gödel.** Arithmetic talks about numbers; numbers can encode formulas and
  proofs; so a sentence of arithmetic can state a property of its own code.
- **Escher, *Drawing Hands*.** Each drawn hand draws the other, so the picture
  contains what appears to be its own maker.
- **Bach, the endlessly rising canon** of the *Musical Offering*. Each
  repetition modulates upward, yet after six steps the piece is back in its
  opening key.
- **Consciousness.** Hofstadter's own proposal is that the self is a pattern
  in which a symbolic system's model of itself takes part in the activity it
  models.

### Defined by example, never formally

Hofstadter had a concept that he hoped would explain intelligence, or one form
of it, and he defined it case by case: Gödel, then Escher, then Bach, then the
self. He never arrived at a formal definition. That is the reading this vault
starts from, and it explains the shape of everything that follows. The
juxtaposition of the three is itself an [[analogy]]: a structure recognised
across logic, drawing and music. An analogy can be strong without showing that
the three share a theorem.

Two responses to the missing definition were tried in the discussion.

- **Sort the examples instead of forcing one definition.** The result is the
  [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]], eight classes that overlap and are not all
  strange loops.
- **Take the one precise case as the reference.** Gödel numbering and the
  incompleteness proof are the only example that is fully mathematical, so the
  formal definitions are modelled on it. The results are collected in
  [[definitions-of-strange-loop]].

### What it is not

An early step in the discussion equated strange loops with any system that
iteratively modifies itself. That was withdrawn as too loose: if it held,
every dynamic system would be a strange loop. A thermostat, a recursive
function and a document that rewrites another document all loop, and none of
them has to cross a level to do it. The key is the unexpected collapse of the
separation between object level and meta level, not dynamism as such.

Nor is a strange loop simply [[diagonalization]]. Gödel's sentence is built by
diagonalization and is a strange loop. Escher's hands and Bach's canon are
strange loops with no diagonal lemma in them. Cantor's diagonal argument is
diagonalization and is not a strange loop. The two notions overlap in exactly
one of the canonical examples.

### The definition this vault works with

The working formulation reached in the discussion is: a strange loop is a
self-referential structure arising through representational level crossing,
and Gödelian diagonalization provides a canonical formal realization of it.

The preferred longer form is:

> A strange loop is a reflective structure in which a system becomes an object
> of its own representation, allowing it to describe, interpret or reconstruct
> aspects of itself, potentially exposing intrinsic limitations of its
> descriptive or reasoning framework.

The word *potentially* carries weight. Without it the definition would claim
that every self-referential cycle is incomplete or inconsistent, which is
false. Limitation is something a strange loop may expose, as Gödel's does, and
not something every strange loop has.

## Relations

- Its defining feature is [[level-crossing]] {relation: uses}: without a crossing there is only a cycle.
- It presupposes the distinction of [[object-and-meta-level]] {relation: uses}, which the loop then collapses.
- It is a kind of [[self-reference]] {relation: is-a}, though not every self-reference is a strange loop.
- It is not the same as a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: feedback stays on one level.
- It is not the same as [[recursion]] {relation: distinguished-from}: recursion descends to smaller instances and stops.
- It is not the same as [[self-similarity]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a fractal resembles itself without referring to itself.
- It overlaps with [[diagonalization]] {relation: distinguished-from}: diagonalization is a proof method, a strange loop an interpretive structure.
- Its sharpest formal instance is the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: example-of}.
- The refinements proposed in this vault are collected in [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: generalizes}.
- It is one region of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}, which sorts the examples without defining the term.
- Hofstadter's way of introducing it is an [[analogy]] {relation: uses} across logic, drawing and music.
- It is the subject of the question [[strange-loop-meaning]] {relation: answers}.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[level-crossing]].** The step in which something at one level of
  description is treated as belonging to another. It is what separates a
  strange loop from a plain cycle.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence of arithmetic that is provably equivalent
  to the statement of its own unprovability. It is the one example in which
  every part of the loop can be written down exactly.
- **[[feedback-loop]].** A cycle in which output influences later input. It is
  the concept most often mistaken for a strange loop, including in descriptions
  of executable documents.
- **[[definitions-of-strange-loop]].** Seven candidate definitions, from
  Hofstadter's original to an epistemically bounded one, ordered by what each
  includes or leaves out.

